<Ui>
    <!--
        Interface\FrameXML\ColorPickerFrame.xml, transcribed — the shared colour dialog. Line
        numbers below are that file's (336 lines, XML only; it ships no `.lua`, its whole script is
        inline and the one function that drives it, `UIDropDownMenuButton_OpenColorPicker`, lives in
        UIDropDownMenu.lua).

        WHY IT IS HERE, in the arc's own terms: **86 of the 218 corpus addons reach
        `ColorPickerFrame`, 80 reach `OpacitySliderFrame`** — and 1207's lesson applies before those
        numbers do, so read the lines. 64 of the files are copies of `Dewdrop-2.0.lua`, which does
        not roll its own picker: it drives THIS one, with the exact block below (Dewdrop-2.0.lua
        l.425-465, verbatim from a corpus copy):

            ColorPickerFrame.func        = function() … ColorPickerFrame:GetColorRGB() …
                                                       OpacitySliderFrame:GetValue() … end
            ColorPickerFrame.hasOpacity  = this.hasOpacity
            ColorPickerFrame.opacityFunc = ColorPickerFrame.func
            ColorPickerFrame.opacity     = 1 - this.opacity
            ColorPickerFrame:SetColorRGB(this.r, this.g, this.b)
            ColorPickerFrame.cancelFunc  = function() … end
            ShowUIPanel(ColorPickerFrame)

        Dewdrop is *the* Ace2 menu library, so that block is how EVERY colour option in EVERY Ace2
        addon's config menu opens. On top of it sit ~14 addons that name the frame directly
        (TipBuddy, CT_UnitFrames, CT_RaidAssist, CooldownCount, PowerAuras, Bongos_Options,
        MikScrollingBattleText, EQL3, RangeColor, ColorPickerPlus, QuestHistory, Radar,
        IgniteStatus, Bagnon_Core), and `AceConsole-2.0.lua` l.1402-1406, which does
        `ColorPickerOkayButton:GetScript("OnClick")` and chains it — so the two BUTTON names are API,
        not decoration.

        The widget itself (`<ColorSelect>`, `SetColorRGB`/`GetColorRGB`/`OnColorSelect`) is
        engine-side, in `benilla-ui/src/script/colorselect.rs` — read its module doc before touching
        anything colour-valued here. Two things live there that are not obvious and are byte-verified
        (wow-re `system/ui/scratch/colorselect-color-law.md`, a §5 cross-check dispatched from this
        repo for exactly this window): the widget's state is **HSV floats**, not RGB, and the
        set→read round trip is deliberately **not** the identity — 9.75 % of colours come back one
        step low on a channel, and the FrameXML `GetColorRGB … SetColorRGB` idiom ratchets. It also
        says why the widget could not be a few methods hung on this one frame (TipBuddy declares two
        `<ColorSelect>` frames of its own).

        ── THE ONE THING THIS WINDOW CANNOT DO, AND IT IS THE BIG ONE ──────────────────────────────
        **There is no colour wheel, so there is no way to CHOOSE a colour in it yet.** The reference
        ends its `<ColorSelect>` with four sub-elements this file does not carry (ref l.186-221):

            <ColorWheelTexture name="ColorPickerWheel"/>   128x128, TOPLEFT (16, -32)
            <ColorWheelThumbTexture/>  10x10, Interface\Buttons\UI-ColorPicker-Buttons,
                                       TexCoords 0 / 0.15625 / 0 / 0.625
            <ColorValueTexture/>       32x128, LEFT of ColorPickerWheel's RIGHT at (24, 0)
            <ColorValueThumbTexture/>  48x14, same sheet, TexCoords 0.25 / 1.0 / 0 / 0.875

        Look at what the first and third do NOT have: a `file=`. The hue wheel and the value strip
        are **generated by the client**, not loaded — there is no BLP anywhere in the MPQ chain that
        is the colour wheel, only the two little thumb markers that ride on top of it. Rendering
        them is an app-renderer job (an HSV wheel + a value ramp drawn for a `FrameKind::ColorSelect`
        rect), not a FrameXML one, so the elements are omitted rather than shipped inert — their
        geometry is recorded above, and dropping them back in is the day that renderer exists.
        (RF-28 has the loader hooks: `<ColorWheelTexture>` `0x78b580`, `<ColorWheelThumbTexture>`
        `0x78b850`, `<ColorValueTexture>` `0x78b8a0`, `<ColorValueThumbTexture>` `0x78ba90` — and the
        INTERACTION half is already carved too, so that day is cheaper than it looks: wow-re's
        `colorselect-color-law.md` §5 has the press/drag handler verbatim — a click jumps the colour
        immediately, `H = (atan2(ny, nx) + π)·180/π`, `S = min(radius, 1)`, `V = clamp((y − bottom) /
        (top − bottom), 0, 1)`, all raw f32 straight into the widget's HSV, no quantizer.)

        Everything ELSE works, and it is most of what the corpus actually exercises: an addon sets a
        colour and the swatch shows it, the opacity slider is live and drives `opacityFunc` on every
        drag, Okay commits through `func` + `opacityFunc`, Cancel restores through `cancelFunc`, and
        both buttons are real named buttons whose `OnClick` an addon can fetch and chain. The
        reference's own geometry is kept exactly — including the 128x128 gap on the left where the
        wheel belongs, and the 365-wide / 305-wide `OnShow` swap around it — because half a window at
        the right size is honest and a re-designed window is not.

        OTHER DEVIATIONS, each forced:
        · **`<OnKeyDown>` (ref l.177-184) is not transcribed** — this engine gives plain frames no
          raw key events (SCRIPT_KINDS has no `OnKeyDown`), the same wall AddonList.xml hit
          (decision 1197). Its ESCAPE arm is `HideUIPanel(this) + cancelFunc(previousValues)`, i.e.
          exactly what the Cancel button does, so the arm is carried in UiPanels.xml's ESC chain as
          `ColorPickerCancelButton:Click()` — the reference's own idiom for this (its game-menu
          chain cancel-clicks `OptionsFrameCancel` the same way). ESC therefore CANCELS, it does not
          merely hide, which is the half people get wrong.
        · **`OpacityFrame` (ref l.223-335) is not transcribed.** The second frame in the reference's
          file: a standalone 80x180 opacity popup with its own slider and a fullscreen
          click-off-to-close button, used by FloatingChatFrame's chat-window alpha. **Zero corpus
          callers** and no benilla consumer, so it waits for its first customer (decision 1195). Its
          globals are `OpacityFrame`, `OpacityFrameSlider`, `OpacityFrameCloseButton`, and
          `OpacityFrame.opacityFunc`/`.saveOpacityFunc`.
        · **The unguarded `ColorPickerFrame.func()` in Okay's OnClick (ref l.89) is KEPT unguarded.**
          It is one of the few places quirk-for-quirk beats tidying: an addon that opened this window
          without setting `func` errors in the real client too, and a guard here would swallow that
          into a window that silently does nothing.
        · `<ColorSelect>` carries no `<Layers>` `hidden` art beyond the reference's three regions;
          `ColorSwatch` and `ColorPickerFrameHeader` keep their unprefixed reference names, which is
          required — `ColorPickerFrame.xml`'s own `<OnColorSelect>` addresses `ColorSwatch` by name,
          and so would any addon that re-skins the window.
    -->

    <Script><![CDATA[
        -- ref GlobalStrings.lua l.770 / l.132 / l.1458 (enUS), in the `X = X or "…"` FALLBACK form
        -- this file set uses throughout: the app runs the real localized GlobalStrings.lua off the
        -- patch chain BEFORE the shipped XML, so these only bind on a surface without it (a headless
        -- test). `text="COLOR_PICKER"` / `text="OKAY"` / `text="CANCEL"` below are global-string
        -- KEYS, not literals — the loader resolves each through the Lua global (rf28 `0x703bf0`).
        COLOR_PICKER = COLOR_PICKER or "Color Picker"
        OKAY = OKAY or "Okay"
        CANCEL = CANCEL or "Cancel"
    ]]></Script>

    <!-- ref l.3-9. 365 wide is the WITH-opacity width; OnShow narrows it to 305 when an addon did
         not ask for the slider. `movable="true"` is the reference's (this engine only honours it
         once something calls StartMoving, which nothing here does — same as the reference, whose
         frame has no drag handler either: the attribute is there for addons). -->
    <ColorSelect name="ColorPickerFrame" toplevel="true" parent="UIParent" movable="true" enableMouse="true" enableKeyboard="true" hidden="true">
        <Size><AbsDimension x="365" y="200"/></Size>
        <Anchors><Anchor point="CENTER"/></Anchors>
        <Backdrop bgFile="Interface\DialogFrame\UI-DialogBox-Background" edgeFile="Interface\DialogFrame\UI-DialogBox-Border" tile="true">
            <BackgroundInsets><AbsInset left="11" right="12" top="12" bottom="11"/></BackgroundInsets>
            <TileSize><AbsValue val="32"/></TileSize>
            <EdgeSize><AbsValue val="32"/></EdgeSize>
        </Backdrop>
        <Layers>
            <Layer level="ARTWORK">
                <!-- ref l.23-35. The live preview: a 32x32 solid quad repainted from OnColorSelect.
                     Unprefixed name, the reference's own — see the header. -->
                <Texture name="ColorSwatch">
                    <Size><AbsDimension x="32" y="32"/></Size>
                    <Anchors><Anchor point="TOPLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="225" y="-32"/></Offset></Anchor></Anchors>
                    <Color r="1.0" g="1.0" b="1.0" a="1.0"/>
                </Texture>
                <!-- ref l.36-47. The same header plate the game menu and every dialog wears. -->
                <Texture name="ColorPickerFrameHeader" file="Interface\DialogFrame\UI-DialogBox-Header">
                    <Size><AbsDimension x="256" y="64"/></Size>
                    <Anchors><Anchor point="TOP"><Offset><AbsDimension x="0" y="12"/></Offset></Anchor></Anchors>
                </Texture>
                <!-- ref l.48-56. Unnamed in the reference too. -->
                <FontString inherits="GameFontNormal" text="COLOR_PICKER">
                    <Anchors>
                        <Anchor point="TOP" relativeTo="ColorPickerFrameHeader"><Offset><AbsDimension x="0" y="-14"/></Offset></Anchor>
                    </Anchors>
                </FontString>
            </Layer>
        </Layers>
        <Frames>
            <!-- ref l.60-77. Cancel is declared FIRST because Okay anchors to it. Its OnClick is the
                 reference's, verbatim: hide, then hand the addon back what it had. -->
            <Button name="ColorPickerCancelButton" inherits="GameMenuButtonTemplate" text="CANCEL">
                <Anchors>
                    <Anchor point="BOTTOMRIGHT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="-10" y="10"/></Offset></Anchor>
                </Anchors>
                <Scripts>
                    <OnClick>
                        HideUIPanel(this:GetParent());
                        if ( ColorPickerFrame.cancelFunc ) then
                            ColorPickerFrame.cancelFunc(ColorPickerFrame.previousValues);
                        end
                        PlaySound("igMainMenuOptionCheckBoxOn");
                    </OnClick>
                </Scripts>
            </Button>
            <!-- ref l.78-96. `func()` unguarded, `opacityFunc()` guarded — the reference's own
                 asymmetry, kept (header). AceConsole-2.0 fetches THIS handler with
                 `ColorPickerOkayButton:GetScript("OnClick")` and calls it from its own replacement,
                 so the name and the script slot are both load-bearing. -->
            <Button name="ColorPickerOkayButton" inherits="GameMenuButtonTemplate" text="OKAY">
                <Anchors>
                    <Anchor point="RIGHT" relativeTo="ColorPickerCancelButton" relativePoint="LEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="0" y="0"/></Offset></Anchor>
                </Anchors>
                <Scripts>
                    <OnClick>
                        HideUIPanel(this:GetParent());
                        ColorPickerFrame.func();
                        if ( ColorPickerFrame.opacityFunc ) then
                            ColorPickerFrame.opacityFunc();
                        end
                        PlaySound("igMainMenuOptionCheckBoxOn");
                    </OnClick>
                </Scripts>
            </Button>
            <!-- ref l.97-158. A real Slider, so it drags: 0..1 in 0.01 steps, VERTICAL, seated to
                 the RIGHT of the swatch. Its `$parentText` label is EMPTY in the reference (only
                 OpacityFrame's copy carries text="OPACITY") — transcribed as written, not
                 "improved". Note the sense: 1 is FULLY OPAQUE, and every caller inverts
                 (`ColorPickerFrame.opacity = 1 - this.opacity`, and Dewdrop's func reads back
                 `1 - OpacitySliderFrame:GetValue()`). -->
            <Slider name="OpacitySliderFrame" orientation="VERTICAL" minValue="0" maxValue="1" valueStep="0.01" defaultValue="1">
                <Size><AbsDimension x="16" y="128"/></Size>
                <Anchors>
                    <Anchor point="TOPLEFT" relativeTo="ColorSwatch" relativePoint="TOPRIGHT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="32" y="0"/></Offset></Anchor>
                </Anchors>
                <Backdrop bgFile="Interface\Buttons\UI-SliderBar-Background" edgeFile="Interface\Buttons\UI-SliderBar-Border" tile="true">
                    <EdgeSize><AbsValue val="8"/></EdgeSize>
                    <TileSize><AbsValue val="8"/></TileSize>
                    <BackgroundInsets><AbsInset left="3" right="3" top="6" bottom="6"/></BackgroundInsets>
                </Backdrop>
                <Layers>
                    <Layer level="ARTWORK">
                        <FontString name="$parentText" inherits="GameFontNormalSmall">
                            <Anchors><Anchor point="BOTTOM" relativePoint="TOP"/></Anchors>
                        </FontString>
                        <FontString inherits="GameFontHighlightSmall" text="0%">
                            <Anchors>
                                <Anchor point="BOTTOMLEFT" relativePoint="BOTTOMRIGHT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="2" y="3"/></Offset></Anchor>
                            </Anchors>
                        </FontString>
                        <FontString inherits="GameFontHighlightSmall" text="100%">
                            <Anchors>
                                <Anchor point="TOPLEFT" relativePoint="TOPRIGHT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="-2" y="3"/></Offset></Anchor>
                            </Anchors>
                        </FontString>
                    </Layer>
                </Layers>
                <Scripts>
                    <OnValueChanged>
                        if ( ColorPickerFrame.opacityFunc ) then
                            ColorPickerFrame.opacityFunc();
                        end
                    </OnValueChanged>
                </Scripts>
                <ThumbTexture file="Interface\Buttons\UI-SliderBar-Button-Vertical">
                    <Size><AbsDimension x="32" y="32"/></Size>
                </ThumbTexture>
            </Slider>
        </Frames>
        <Scripts>
            <!-- ref l.161-170. The opacity slider is opt-in per caller, and the window narrows by
                 the slider's 60 units when nobody asked for it. -->
            <OnShow>
                if ( this.hasOpacity ) then
                    OpacitySliderFrame:Show();
                    OpacitySliderFrame:SetValue(this.opacity);
                    this:SetWidth(365);
                else
                    OpacitySliderFrame:Hide();
                    this:SetWidth(305);
                end
            </OnShow>
            <!-- ref l.171-176. The widget's own slot, fired by SetColorRGB with the colour the
                 widget now holds (colorselect.rs). This is the ONLY thing that paints the swatch —
                 no caller touches it — and it is also where an addon's live-preview `func` runs. -->
            <OnColorSelect>
                ColorSwatch:SetTexture(arg1, arg2, arg3);
                if ( this.func ) then
                    this.func();
                end
            </OnColorSelect>
        </Scripts>
    </ColorSelect>
</Ui>
